        "geoBonBiome": null,
        "geology": "The Amazon catchment covers 6.2 million sq.km encompassing various soils and lithologies. The Amazon basin is composed by three major morpho-structural units : i) the Brazilian and Guyana cratonic shields, ii) the Altiplano, Oriental Cordillera and Subandean zone of the Andes and iii) the lowlands. The cratonic shields cover about 44% of the Amazonian basin area and are composed of strongly weathered ancient folded and metamorphosed igneous rocks as well as Precambrian to Mesozoic sedimentary rocks. The Andes account for 11% of the Amazonian basin area and are made up of Paleozoic to Meso-Cenozoic metamorphic, igneous and sedimentary rocks. The Amazonian lowlands cover about 45% of the Amazonian basin and are composed of Cenozoic sediments",
        "hydrology": "The Amazon Basin is the biggest river basin in the world. This basin flows into the Atlantic Ocean at approximately 6.6 1012 m³ yr-1, and this volume is approximately 16% of the world?s fresh water. The catchment is located across the two hemispheres which makes that the flood pattern varies as a function of latitude. Therefore, the Amazon River mainstem exhibit a low variability with a well defined monomodal flood starting on November and with a flood peak in June.",
